<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Auch</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Elimberris, Augusta Auscorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Auch is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gers">Gers</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. Called Elimberris by the Aquitanian Ausci and Augusta Auscorum by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, it became the seat of the archbishops of Gascony in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Its Gothic cathedral of Sainte-Marie is known for its carved choir stalls and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stained-glass">stained glass</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/auch.jpg" alt="Auch" width="320" height="286" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hardouin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Auch1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Auch is a commune in southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occitania">Occitania</a>, and the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gers">Gers</a> department. It lies in the Gers valley, roughly in the centre of the department, 69 kilometres west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulouse">Toulouse</a>. The commune covers 7,248 hectares, the third largest in the department, in a hillside landscape between 115 and 281 metres above sea level.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gers">Gers</a>, a tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garonne">Garonne</a>, divides the town. The upper town on the left bank, built on a hill, is the site of the medieval city and most of the ancient monuments, and the lower town lies on the plain on the right bank. Narrow, steep medieval streets called pousterles link the upper town to the river, and a monumental staircase was inaugurated in 1863. The lower town was flooded several times, in 1897 and in 1952, before the course of the Gers was recalibrated after the floods of 1977.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Auch is a very ancient town, noted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> during their conquest of the area in the 50s BCE, when it was settled by an Aquitanian tribe they called the Ausci. Its name for the town was Climberrum or Elimberris, tentatively explained from the Iberian iltir, meaning town, and a cognate of the Basque berri, new. The Romans renamed it Augusta Auscorum, Augusta of the Ausci. It was one of the twelve civitates of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novempopulania">Novempopulana</a> and became the provincial capital after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eauze">Eauze</a> in 409. The word Augusta was eventually dropped, and the name evolved into the Gascon Aush and the French Auch.</p><p>In 732, during Abdul Rahman's advance towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-invasion-of-gaul">Umayyad invasion of Gaul</a>, the town was supposedly moved across the river to much of its present site to avoid destruction. It became the seat of a Catholic archdiocese that lasted until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, and its archbishops claimed the title of Primate of Aquitaine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novempopulania">Novempopulana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. The Hôtel de Ville was completed in 1778.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Auch” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They were among the Aquitani, whose language was related to Basque; the name of their town, <b>Elimberris</b>, is usually read as Basque-like 'new town', comparable with Iliberris in Iberia.</blockquote><a href="/state/ausci">Ausci</a></li><li><blockquote>His descendants ruled the hill country around <b>Auch</b> and Lectoure and gradually gathered neighbouring counties such as Fezensac and Rodez.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-armagnac">County of Armagnac</a></li></ul></section>
